   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### Result (2026-10-02)\n**Single-Q forms, checked numerically.** Tested at $t=62/35$, finite twist, on all 18 states of $(2,2,2)$, $(3,2,1)$ and $(3,3,0)$, for every $c$ and $j$, to $\\sim10^{-50}$:\n$$T_{(1,1)}(\\theta_1-2h)=3h^2\\chi_3(\\theta_{12}-h)(\\theta_{12}-3h)\\,r,\\qquad T_{(2,1)}(\\theta_1-2h)=-3h^3\\chi_3(\\theta_{12}-h)^2(\\theta_{12}-3h)\\,\\rho\\,r,$$\nwith $r=\\frac{Q_c(\\theta_1-h)}{Q_c(\\theta_1-2h)}$ (direct, any $c$) and $\\rho=\\frac{Q^j(\\theta_1)}{Q^j(\\theta_1-h)}$ (dual, any $j$). For the top state, $r=1/z_1$ and $\\rho=z_3$.\n\n**The clean combination, derived.** It is exact at leading order and independent of $\\varphi,\\omega$; Section 1 derives it at $h=1$, and $h$ is restored by homogeneity:\n$$\\mathcal C=T_{(2,1)}(\\theta_1-2h)+h(2\\theta_{12}-3h)\\,T_{(1,1)}(\\theta_1-2h),\\qquad \\text{Q-number}=(\\mathcal C^B-\\mathcal C^{\\rm top})\\,\\langle\\Omega|\\Psi_B\\rangle.$$\nIn Q's, dropping the state-independent factor $3h^3\\chi_3(\\theta_{12}-h)(\\theta_{12}-3h)$:\n$$\\boxed{\\;\\Big\\{\\,r\\big[(2\\theta_{12}-3h)-(\\theta_{12}-h)\\,\\rho\\big]\\;-\\;z_1^{-1}\\big[(2\\theta_{12}-3h)-(\\theta_{12}-h)\\,z_3\\big]\\Big\\}\\,\\langle\\Omega|\\Psi_B\\rangle\\;}$$\nwith $\\langle\\Omega|\\Psi_B\\rangle$ the $6\\times6$ determinant formula.\n\n**Why it is clean.**\n- The top subtraction removes the $O(\\varepsilon)$ contamination identically, because $\\Omega$ lives in the 27.\n- What is left is $\\varepsilon^2(W-T^{\\rm top}\\Omega)_1\\cdot\\psi_1$.\n- That is subleading for $[4,1,1]$, $[3,3,0]$ and the octets, whose signals are $O(\\varepsilon)$.\n- For the singlet, signal $O(\\varepsilon^2)$, the ratio $h(2\\theta_{12}-3h)$ cancels it: $K_{(1,1)}/S_{(2,1)}=-\\tfrac13$ and $K_{(2,1)}/S_{(2,1)}=\\tfrac{2t-3}{3}$.\n\nSo a single combination gives clean CG data for all six HW states of $[2,1,0]^2$.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## 3. Can a rescaling of the monodromy remove the $t$-dependence?\n**How a rescaling acts.** Rescale $T(u)\\to T(u)/g(u)$ by a scalar function. A quantum minor of size $a$ picks up $\\prod_{k=1}^{a}g(u-(k-1)h)$, and a CBR-fused $T_\\lambda(u)$ picks up $\\prod_{(r,c)\\in\\lambda}g(u+(c-r)h)$. At $u=\\theta_1-2h$:\n- $T_{(1,1)}\\to T_{(1,1)}/[g(u)g(u-h)]$;\n- $T_{(2,1)}\\to T_{(2,1)}/[g(u+h)g(u)g(u-h)]$.\n\nThe clean combination $T_{(2,1)}+h(2\\theta_{12}-3h)T_{(1,1)}$ becomes, up to an overall factor, $\\tilde T_{(2,1)}+\\frac{h(2\\theta_{12}-3h)}{g(\\theta_1-h)}\\tilde T_{(1,1)}$. So a rescaling changes only **one number**, $g(\\theta_1-h)$.\n\n**What would work.** For a $t$-independent coefficient we need $g(\\theta_1-h)\\propto 2\\theta_{12}-3h$. For $g$ a product of site factors $\\prod_s(u-\\theta_s+a_sh)$, the site-2 factor at $\\theta_1-h$ is $\\theta_{12}+(a_2-1)h$, which forces $a_2=-\\tfrac12$. The cell evaluates the coefficient for $g=\\nu_1,\\nu_2,\\nu_3$, for the half-shifted $g(u)=\\prod_s(u-\\theta_s-h/2)$, and for the localization factors (the $f$-normalized basis). Here $h=1$, and the coefficients come from the exact $K$'s of Section 1.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* exact clean coefficient in a rescaled basis: only g(θ1-h) = g(u1+1) survives, c = -(K21/K11)/g(θ1-h); h = 1, u1 = tt - 2 *)\nKr = sing1[\"21\", \"K2\"]/sing1[\"11\", \"K2\"];\ncoefFor[g_] := Factor[-Kr/g[u1 + 1]];\nnuS[n_][x_] := Product[x - thS[[s]] + Λ[[n]], {s, 2}];\ncands = {{\"none (raw T's)\", 1 &}, {\"ν1\", nuS[1]}, {\"ν2\", nuS[2]}, {\"ν3\", nuS[3]}, {\"ν3(u-h/2) = ∏_s(u-θ_s-h/2)\", Product[# - thS[[s]] - 1/2, {s, 2}] &}};\nresc = Table[{c[[1]], Factor[c[[2]][u1 + 1]], If[TrueQ[c[[2]][u1 + 1] == 0], \"singular: g vanishes at θ1-h\", coefFor[c[[2]]]]}, {c, cands}];\nGrid[Join[{{\"rescaling g(u)\", \"g(θ1-h)\", \"clean combination T~21 + c T~11 :  c =\"}}, resc,\n   {{\"f-normalized X's (localization factors)\", \"–\", Factor[-Kr fS[\"11\"]/fS[\"21\"]]}}], Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
      {
       "data": "<div class=\"wl-output-block\"><div class=\"wl-output-header\" style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;width:100%;min-height:22px;\" data-session-epoch=\"942214\" data-output-id=\"85\" data-out-n=\"901\" data-sub-idx=\"6\" data-output-format=\"WLLatex\" data-output-is-graphics=\"0\" data-cell-idx=\"9\" data-sub-start=\"6\" data-sub-end=\"7\"><span style=\"font-size:10px;color:#888;margin-right:8px;\">Out[901]=</span></div><div class=\"wl-output-content\"><div class=\"vscode-wolfram-wllatex-prerendered\" data-page-width-em=\"27\" data-latex-b64=\"\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:8.5904em;vertical-align:-4.0252em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:4.5652em;\"><span style=\"top:-6.5252em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:6.5252em;\"></span><span class=\"mtable\"><span class=\"vertical-separator\" style=\"height:8.5504em;border-right-width:0.04em;border-right-style:solid;margin:0 -0.02em;vertical-align:-4.0252em;\"></span><span class=\"arraycolsep\" style=\"width:0.5em;\"></span><span class=\"col-align-c\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:4.5252em;\"><span style=\"top:-6.6852em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">rescaling g(u)</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-5.4852em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">none (raw T’s)</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-4.2801em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.06366em;\">ν</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span><